Z||000013||KissMySSFiero||09-18-2001||02:26 PM||||Archie are you still working on the setup for the 88's?   id be the first to buy if your done.   =Þ    now im gettin another fiero thats an 84 so i can the big breaks on that too.  gotta love  to stop.  no matter how fast you go,  if you cant stop your gonna die.  well mabye not.  i once had a ford tempo that i had to use the curb a few times to stop it.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000014||Slammed Fiero||09-18-2001||04:26 PM||||I suggest to anyone considering this swap to just buy it from archie. Even with the conversion rate withthe canadian dollar I could have saved a few bucks. I used the webpage with the thought that is had complete instructions. There are a few little tricks that are not mentioned. namely how the rears go together.<P>Pads are the same all around , if your so inclined it is easy to upgrade to bigger calipers upfront , Baer makes a direct replacement for the 89-92 camaro that is a 4 piston unit. Gm also had a hi performance caliper for 1LE camaro's that  believe was aluminum.<P>The setup is heavier than stock , but I personally got he feeling of a more planted ride over the stock units.<P>I suggest this setup to anyone , if I get another fiero I would put it on a daily driver.<P>Archie , did you use a welded bead in the inside of the front rotor?? I used an insert tack welded and balanced , thinking it over I wish I had just got the insert welded to the front hub , that way I can swap stock rotors without the machining.<P>I suggest this swap to anyone!! If you need any help and your in the midst of the swap feel free to email me, I have the scared knuckles that prove I know how it goes together.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||09-18-2001||05:46 PM||||i'm always leary of swaps like these. i admit that the numbers you show are more than impressive, but is the car still balanced on corners and such? i'd be worried about stability... any adverse reactions?||||reg||5
Z||000016||Slammed Fiero||09-18-2001||06:35 PM||||Initially the car is the same as the factory intended.<P>I only enlarged the size of the rotors , thus the balance of the car is not upset. It's not an elaborate system that requires bias adjusters to compensate for a smaller rotor on one end of the car.<P>Due to the mild increase in unsprung weight the car feels even more stable than stock.<P>I used a blazer master cylinder in the conversionto maintain pedal pressure. I have put around 700 miles on the car since the conversion and can't see driving without it. I was out in my moms 95.5.0ltr mustang and was amazed at how much better the fiero brakes.<P>If anyone is ever in the area and wants a ride , feel free to give me a call. I have performed 5 or 6 exabition stops for passengers , this basically entails running it up to 100mph and standing on the brakes. Since the 700miles I have had the wheels off the car and checked everything , still perfect.<P>I would be learly of systems that used unequal size rotors without a proportioning valve to compensate for the stronger end of the car. <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000017||KissMySSFiero||09-18-2001||07:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B><P>Archie , did you use a welded bead in the inside of the front rotor?? I used an insert tack welded and balanced , thinking it over I wish I had just got the insert welded to the front hub , that way I can swap stock rotors without the machining.<P>JM </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>what do you mean by the welded bead?  im not familiar at all with pre88 suspension so i dontknow what to look at on the car.  is this somehting that can be done with a regular welder or would it have to be something to take to a machine shop?  do you have any pics of the install while you were working on it?  what did you use for break lines and where did you get them?  <BR><P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000018||VISCERAL||09-25-2001||01:59 PM||||What's the part # of the master cyl? IRON or Aluminum?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Slammed Fiero||09-25-2001||02:53 PM||||I have been away for the weekend.<P>Welded bead: This goes in the bore hole of the front rotor to align it correctly with the Fiero hubs. I used  steel insert instead of the welded bead approach.<P>As for the master cylinder , it came from a 94 Blazer , it's Iron. I used the fiero resivoir so that  can still put my sunroof upfront.<P>I suggest to anyone planning to use these brakes for autocross to get a Wilwood or tilton balancer. They stop amazingly from highr speeds , but are hard to modulate at lower speeds and under hard braking situations. a cheap remedy is to put regular pads upfront and expensive pads in rear , this cured my problems , but I ordered a balancer to straighten everything out a little more professionally. <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000020||VISCERAL||09-25-2001||04:33 PM||||Thanks I was going nuts to figure it out. Archies on vacation. The kit info says 2218, carquest has no such number and the closest match for 92-94 chevy 1/2 ton, blazer, 2wd p/u is 2219 and its iron. The kit info said aluminum so I was freakin. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000021||jelly2m8||09-25-2001||04:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by VISCERAL:<BR><B>Thanks I was going nuts to figure it out. Archies on vacation. The kit info says 2218, carquest has no such number and the closest match for 92-94 chevy 1/2 ton, blazer, 2wd p/u is 2219 and its iron. The kit info said aluminum so I was freakin. Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I used the 93 2wd full sized blazer MC on the V8. Its Aluminum, and looks alomost exactly like the stock MC, just a shade bigger in size. I got mine from UAP / NAPA.<P> I'll see if i can find the slip with the part number as the box went back for the core charge with a fiero MC in there............ hehe||||reg||1
Z||000022||Slammed Fiero||09-25-2001||04:54 PM||||VISCERAL:<P>Get a balancer , Get a balancer , Get a balancer.<P>Not needed for most drivers out there , but the system has the same problem as stock fiero brakes , you can lock up the fronts a little to easy. even with 225/17's you can lock them up at 70mph if you try hard enough.<P>balancer is  cheap ($45) and I have the diagram to hook it into a fiero if your interested<P>JM<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||VISCERAL||09-25-2001||08:47 PM||||Thanks Jelly2m8, Btw how much?||||reg||1
